Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT vs XML: structure and validation without verbosity
	Metadata:
		Last modif: 2026-09-19
		Description: STXT and XML compared with examples: the same tree with and without closing tags, escapes and entities, attributes versus children, and an XSD versus an STXT template.

	Header: @STXT@ vs XML

	Assert >>
		XML is the closest format to @STXT@: a tree of named nodes, namespaces and schemas.
		@STXT@ aims for the same, in a document that can be written and read by hand.

	Subheader: The same content, twice

	Content >>
		The header of an article in XML:

	Xml >>
		<article xmlns="http://example.com/articles">
			<title>Modern Software &amp; Architecture</title>
			<published>2026-09-01</published>
			<author email="ana@example.com">Ana López</author>
			<summary>
				A survey of monoliths, microservices
				&lt;and everything in between&gt;.
			</summary>
		</article>

	Content >>
		And in @STXT@:

	Code >>
		Article (com.example.articles): Modern Software & Architecture
			Published: 2026-09-01
			Author: Ana López
				Email: ana@example.com
			Summary >>
				A survey of monoliths, microservices
				<and everything in between>.

	Content >>
		It is the same tree, with the same idea of namespace. The differences are these:

		|                        | XML                                          | @STXT@                                                 |
		| ---------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
		| End of a node          | Closing tag                                  | The indentation                                        |
		| Characters `<` and `&` | Escaped (`&lt;`, `&amp;`) or placed in CDATA | They mean nothing, any character is legal              |
		| Where a datum goes     | In an attribute or in a child element        | Always in a child node                                 |
		| Entities               | Internal and external                        | None                                                   |
		| Schemas                | DTD, XSD, RELAX NG, Schematron               | `@stxt.schema` and `@stxt.template`, written in @STXT@ |

	Subheader: Escapes and entities

	Content >>
		XML reserves `<` and `&` in content. Text is escaped, or wrapped in a CDATA section,
		which in turn cannot contain `]]>`.

		Entities also open two well-known attacks:

		* **External entities (XXE)**: they read files or reach the network.
		* **Entity expansion**: it exhausts memory.

		Hardened parsers disable those features. @STXT@ does not have them: there are no entities,
		references or inclusion ([STXT-SPEC §15](stxt-core-ref#s15)).

	Subheader: Attributes and elements

	Content >>
		XML offers two places for a datum, attribute or child element. Each vocabulary decides,
		and whoever reads it has to look at both:

	Xml >>
		<server name="db1" host="10.0.0.5" port="5432">
			<timeout unit="s">30</timeout>
			<replica host="10.0.0.6"/>
			<replica host="10.0.0.7"/>
		</server>

	Content >>
		In @STXT@ there are only nodes. What was an attribute or an element in XML is here, in both cases, a child:

	Code >>
		Server: db1
			Host: 10.0.0.5
			Port: 5432
			Timeout: 30
				Unit: s
			Replica: 10.0.0.6
			Replica: 10.0.0.7

	Content >>
		XML attributes are more compact on one line. @STXT@ gives that up in exchange for a single form.

	Subheader: Validation

	Content >>
		An XSD for the article from the beginning:

	Xml >>
		<xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
		           targetNamespace="http://example.com/articles"
		           xmlns="http://example.com/articles"
		           elementFormDefault="qualified">
			<xs:element name="article">
				<xs:complexType>
					<xs:sequence>
						<xs:element name="title" type="xs:string"/>
						<xs:element name="published" type="xs:date"/>
						<xs:element name="author" maxOccurs="unbounded">
							<xs:complexType>
								<xs:simpleContent>
									<xs:extension base="xs:string">
										<xs:attribute name="email" type="xs:string" use="required"/>
									</xs:extension>
								</xs:simpleContent>
							</xs:complexType>
						</xs:element>
						<xs:element name="summary" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0"/>
					</xs:sequence>
				</xs:complexType>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:schema>

	Content >>
		And the @STXT@ template, written in the language itself and with the shape of the document it describes:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.articles
			Structure >>
				Article (com.example.articles):
					Published: (1) DATE
					Author: (+)
						Email: (1) EMAIL
					Summary: (?) TEXT

	Content >>
		They are not fully equivalent, and the difference goes in both directions:

		* **The XSD validates more**: the order of the children (`xs:sequence`), patterns, ranges and derived types.
		  @STXT@ schemas do not validate order, and have no regular expressions or conditional rules
		  ([STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC §11](stxt-schema-ref#s11)).
		* **The template checks that `Email` is an email address**, and `xs:string` does not.
		* **The model of the template is closed**: any undeclared child is an error.

	Code >>
		# ERROR: this document does not validate
		Article (com.example.articles): Modern Software & Architecture
			# `September 1st` is not a DATE
			Published: September 1st
			Author: Ana López
				# `ana` is not an EMAIL
				Email: ana
			# `Editor` is not in the template
			Editor: Joan Costa

	Subheader: When to use each

	Content >>
		* **XML**: where its ecosystem is the value. Established vocabularies (DocBook, SVG, ODF, RSS),
		  tooling over XSLT and XPath, and **mixed content**, the inline markup within prose.
		  @STXT@ does not model it: formatted prose goes in a block node, in [Markdown](stxt-vs-markdown).
		* **@STXT@**: in the documents that people write and organizations validate. For example,
		  [corporate documents](use-cases-corporate-docs), [contracts](use-cases-contracts),
		  [RFCs and technical proposals](use-cases-rfcs) or [CMS content](use-cases-cms).

		The short version of this comparison is in the [FAQ](faq#why-not-xml-yaml-or-toml).
